// expect-error: discarded optional result
//
// `?T` exists so a failure cannot be ignored — the checker forces an unwrap at
// every USE site. Throwing the whole optional away was the hole in that
// argument: `rb.push_checked(a);` is exactly `rb.push(a)` once the `?void` is
// dropped, so the one method whose entire purpose is REPORTING overflow
// silently stopped reporting it. Nothing said so at compile time or at runtime.
//
// Corpus cost measured before shipping: 6 discarding sites in the whole tree,
// all of them ghost-handle NEGATIVES. Zero valid programs.
// The escape valve is `rb.push_checked(a) orelse { };` — one line, and it
// states the intent to ignore rather than hiding it.
struct M { u32 v; }
Ring(M, 2) rb;

u32 main() {
    M a; a.v = 1;
    rb.push_checked(a);     // ?void discarded: a full ring is silently ignored
    return 0;
}
